; taint_reject_spec_quantum_return_measure.ll — DECLINE (bd test_C_libtooling-9bhg).
;
; The QUANTUM-verdict twin of taint_accept_spec_classical_return.ll, and the
; fixture that pins the LATE re-ask. A TWO-TU module (caller TU `-include CQ.h`,
; llvm-linked with a plain-C library TU, as tests/e2e/run_slice_multi.sh builds one).
;
; `f` returns `t = h(q, m)`, a specialization site. Here `h(a, k) = a + k`, so the
; clone's return DOES derive from the register: `f` returns a quantum HANDLE and
; `main`'s `cq_measure(f(m))` would read it — bd 3jru's miscompile (on aad63450 the
; measurement was DELETED down the bd 21mg classical-measure path).
;
; `f`'s return taint is SPECIALIZATION-PROVISIONAL until `specialize()` runs, so
; bd 9bhg does not cross it at propagation and the early refusal skips `f`; `main`
; sees `f(m)` as classical in that window. The ONLY thing that refuses this module
; is `declineQuantumReturnCalls` RE-ASKED after `specialize()`, where `t`'s taint is
; the clone's final verdict. Delete that re-ask and this module compiles with the
; user's measurement silently gone. (On 983f9361 the same string came from the
; EARLY slot.)
;
; Library TU (plain C, no CQ.h):
;   int h(int a, int k) { return a + k; }
;
; Caller TU (clang -O1 -include CQ.h):
;   #include <stdlib.h>
;   int h(int a, int k);
;   __attribute__((noinline)) int f(int m) { int q = cq_theta(m, .5); int t = h(q, m); return t; }
;   int main(int argc, char **argv) { int m = atoi(argv[1]); return cq_measure(f(m)); }
;
; Regenerate: clang -O1 -emit-llvm -S each TU as above, then
;   llvm-link -S caller.ll lib.ll -o <this file's body>.
;
; RUN: not %cq-opt -load-pass-plugin %cq-pass -passes=cq-lowering -S %s 2>&1 \
; RUN:   | %filecheck %s
; CHECK: in function main {{.*}}: cq taint escape: quantum value returned across a function boundary
